    "explanation": "To both parties — here is what this case turns on and what it pays. The deviation is real, and it is measured on the GPS trace, not on anyone's recollection. EV-GPS-00513 (313 fixes at a 10-second cadence, no gaps) records 51.86 km between pickup and dropoff against a booked optimal of 38.1 km on the trip record (EV-TRIP-00513). That is an excess of 13.76 km, or 36.1% of optimal — past both POL-RD-01 gates (over 0.8 km and over 20%) and large enough (over 8.0 km) to be a material deviation. The rider's pleaded 'fifty became seventy' is not the measured figure; the figure that decides is 13.76 km, and a bigger pleaded number would not have enlarged the refund, because POL-RD-02's recomputation depends only on the optimal distance and duration. The driver's explanation — a jam near Marina and an app re-route — does not reduce the excess. POL-RD-13 requires such an assertion to be tested against the traffic advisory for this trip and nothing else. The only advisory on file (EV-ADVIS-00513-01, LTA DataMall ADV-20261413-001) is for the MCE westbound and is marked intersects_route: false: it covers a corridor the optimal route never uses, and POL-RD-03 states such an advisory exonerates nothing. Its shortest_lawful_alternative_km is also null, so the 130% cap cannot be computed. The driver's own statement cites no advisory ID, so the assertion carries no weight under POL-RD-08 and the excess stands at the measured 13.76 km. The driver-advocate's alternative submission that the null cap field makes POL-RD-10(5) escalate is rejected: POL-RD-03 is unavailable because the advisory fails its very first requirement (a corridor the optimal route traverses), not because a field is missing; POL-RD-13 gives the contrary reading priority for this cluster, and the driver cited no artifact for POL-RD-10(5) to bite on. No escalation trigger in POL-RD-10 fires. The refund is arithmetic under POL-RD-02. charged_total is 58.09 from the fare breakdown. recomputed_fare = ((3.80 base + 0.75 x 38.1 km + 0.28 x 42.0 min) x surge 1.0 read from fare_breakdown.surge_multiplier) + 0.80 platform fee = 44.935. Refund = 58.09 - 44.935 = 13.155, rounded UP to SGD 13.20. That is 22.6% of the charge, below the 90% that would make it a full refund. Nothing is added for pass-through lines: erp_gantries is empty, tolls are 0.00, and no airport surcharge applies — this trip ends at Changi, and a surcharge on an arriving trip would itself be a billing-integrity defect under POL-RD-09(4); in any event POL-RD-09(1) forbids stacking such a correction on the POL-RD-02 figure. The compulsory POL-RD-06 driver-profile check was run because the deviation is material: driver_profile.deviation_flags_90d is 0 against 250 completed trips in 90 days, so no repeat-deviation pattern is established, no full-refund uplift under POL-RD-06(b) applies, and no driver-conduct referral under POL-RD-06(c) arises. No other clause excuses the excess: the chat log is empty (POL-RD-04), no endpoint was amended and amended_optimal_distance_km is null (POL-RD-05), the vehicle is RYDE_TAXI not RYDE_POOL (POL-RD-12), and the fare model is METERED with no quote (POL-RD-11). There is no offsetting driver claim: distance_charged_km (51.9) is not below the trace distance (51.86), so no POL-RD-04 compensation arises. One open point is recorded here rather than as a referral: the rider's remark about the driver asking which country she was flying to is a conduct concern, but no clause on this record produces a referral for it — POL-RD-06(c) requires an established repeat pattern, which does not exist, and the chat log is empty — so the platform may wish to look at that separately. Result: PARTIAL_REFUND of SGD 13.20 to the rider; the remainder of the SGD 58.09 stands."
